    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>No trial has tested whether swallowing collagen makes a broken bone heal faster.</strong> A narrative review of supplements in adult fracture healing, published in the <em>Journal of Dietary Supplements</em> in 2026, searched this literature and reported that no adult trials directly assess collagen; the protein, vitamin D and calcium evidence is what carries fracture recovery.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nothing directly measured:</strong> the 2026 review found no adult trial of collagen with fracture healing as the outcome. Neither did it find one for omega-3s or creatine.</li>
        <li><strong>What does have evidence:</strong> high-protein supplementation after hip fracture reduced unfavourable outcomes, relative risk 0.78 (99% CI 0.65 to 0.95), in a Cochrane review of 41 trials and 3,881 patients.</li>
        <li><strong>Correct the deficiency, not the trend:</strong> 72% of Indian hip fracture patients were vitamin D deficient in a 2026 cross-sectional study — a bigger lever than any powder.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limitation:</strong> Skelecoll's 500mg Cissus quadrangularis is a third of the 1,500mg a day used in the only human fracture study of hadjod, and that study had nine patients in total.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

