      <h2 id="direct-evidence">Does collagen help a broken bone heal faster?</h2>
      <p>Nobody has measured it properly, so the honest answer is that we do not know — which is not the same as "it does nothing", and not the same as what the supplement pages claim.</p>
      <p>The clearest statement available is a 2026 narrative review of nutraceuticals in adult fracture healing in the <em>Journal of Dietary Supplements</em>. Its authors searched for human trials of protein, amino acids, collagen, calcium, vitamin D, magnesium, zinc, vitamin C, omega-3s and creatine in fracture patients, and reported that no adult trials directly assess collagen, omega-3s or creatine. The human evidence sits almost entirely with protein, amino acids, vitamin D and calcium, with mixed effects on healing and radiographic union.</p>
      <p>Two things get mistaken for that missing evidence. The first is collagen used as a surgical implant: a randomised trial across 18 centres in <em>The Journal of Bone and Joint Surgery</em> in 1997 found no significant difference in union rates between a bovine collagen and calcium phosphate graft material and the patient's own hip bone graft. That is material packed into a bone defect in theatre, and it says nothing about a shot you drink.</p>
      <p>The second is the one small human study seller pages cite as if it settled things: 28 patients, 14 per arm, reporting better bone healing on a specific bioactive collagen peptide, published in <em>Nutrafoods</em>. Fourteen people per group cannot establish an effect, the journal is a supplement-industry title rather than an orthopaedic one, and the 2026 review did not treat it as an answer. Animal work is warmer — a rat study of a collagen and arginine supplement found better callus quality — but rats are not a reason to spend money.</p>

