      <h2 id="what-works">What actually helps a fracture heal?</h2>
      <p>Protein intake, correcting a vitamin D deficiency, enough calcium, not smoking, and doing exactly what your surgeon says about weight-bearing. In that order of evidence, and none of it exotic.</p>
      <p>The strongest single piece of evidence in this field is unglamorous. The Cochrane review of nutritional supplementation for hip fracture aftercare pooled 41 trials and 3,881 patients and found high-protein supplementation associated with fewer unfavourable outcomes, relative risk 0.78 (99% CI 0.65 to 0.95). It found no reduction in deaths and graded the evidence low to very low quality. A 2026 randomised trial in the <em>Journal of Orthopaedic Trauma</em> gave hip fracture patients a creatine and HMB-enriched protein supplement for a month and reported better early mobility and faster albumin normalisation, but its mortality signal spanned 1.12 to 18.26 — too imprecise to act on — and six-month mobility was no different.</p>
      <p>Here is how the common supplements line up when the outcome has to be a fracture healing, not a bone density scan.</p>

