      <p>The largest effect in this whole area is missing from that table, because it is not a supplement. A 2016 systematic review with meta-analysis found smokers carry roughly twice the risk of nonunion after fracture, osteotomy or arthrodesis — odds ratio 2.50 (95% CI 1.73 to 3.61) — rising to a pooled risk ratio of 3.68 in conservatively treated displaced midshaft clavicle fractures. If you smoke and you have a fracture, stopping now beats every bottle in this article.</p>

