      <h2 id="hadjod">Does hadjod work for fractures?</h2>
      <p>The human evidence is two tiny studies in facial bones, at a dose three times what most Indian products contain. Hadjod, or Cissus quadrangularis, is the plant Indian households reach for after a fracture, and the one ingredient here with any traditional claim to bone healing at all.</p>
      <p>The pilot everyone cites was published in <em>AYU</em> in 2015: five maxillofacial fracture patients received Cissus quadrangularis and four did not, at one 500mg capsule three times a day for six weeks. Pain, swelling and fragment mobility were lower in the treated group, with healing further along by day 21. A study in the <em>National Journal of Maxillofacial Surgery</em> in 2013 reported healing time shortened by about two weeks, and a mandibular distraction study reported more osteogenic potential during consolidation.</p>
      <p>Nine patients, then a handful more, all in jaw and facial bone, none in a long bone, none large enough to survive a serious statistical challenge. The animal data is more consistent than the human data, which is the signature of an ingredient nobody has tested properly yet.</p>
      <p>And the dose matters. Those studies used 1,500mg a day. Skelecoll contains 500mg of Cissus quadrangularis — a third of it. That figure is on our label and we are not going to pretend it is a therapeutic fracture dose. The same arithmetic applies to the 300mg of glucosamine HCl in the shot against the 1,500mg used in glucosamine monotherapy trials, which is why <a href="/pages/supplements-for-knee-pain">our guide to knee supplements</a> treats it as a co-factor rather than a headline.</p>

      <h2 id="vitamin-d">Why vitamin D matters more in India</h2>
      <p>Because deficiency is close to the norm in Indian fracture patients, and it is the one fixable thing here with a plausible link to how a bone heals. A 2026 cross-sectional study of Indian hip fracture patients in the <em>Journal of Orthopaedic Case Reports</em> found 72% were vitamin D deficient, with moderate deficiency, 10 to 20 ng/mL, the commonest band; older Indian series have put hypovitaminosis D in fragility hip fracture patients as high as 85.5%.</p>
      <p>Observational and registry data link ongoing deficiency to delayed or failed union — but be careful what follows from that. A phase II randomised, double-blind trial in <em>JBMR Plus</em> in 2023 gave vitamin D3 to women who were <em>not</em> deficient and detected no fracture healing benefit, and a trial of 100,000 IU in long bone fractures found nonunion in 4% of both groups. Topping up someone already replete does not appear to do anything for the fracture.</p>
      <p>So the action is a blood test, then a correction dose your doctor chooses. Skelecoll carries 600 IU of vitamin D3, a maintenance amount and a reasonable background intake; it is not a treatment dose for a documented deficiency and should not be used as one. If you have never had the test, <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">what vitamin D deficiency actually feels like</a> is the place to start, and <a href="/pages/do-calcium-supplements-work">whether calcium supplements do anything for bone</a> covers the other half of that prescription.</p>

      <h2 id="mynourish">The trial that will finally answer this</h2>
      <p>It is registered and it has not reported. MyNOURISH, registration NCT07347236, is a study of hydrolysed collagen supplementation in older adults with fragility fractures, run from Hospital Pengajar Universiti Putra Malaysia. Its registry entry lists nutritional status, physical function, body composition and bone health as the outcomes, with primary completion expected March 2027 and the study finishing June 2027.</p>
      <p>That will be the first time anyone has given collagen to fracture patients and measured what happened. Until it reports, every page telling you collagen heals bones faster is extrapolating from bone density studies in postmenopausal women, from rats, or from fourteen people in a supplement-industry journal. A 2026 scoping review in <em>Bone</em> landed in the same careful place: targeted supplementation could <em>potentially</em> improve osteogenic potential — a sentence about possibility, not proof.</p>
      <p>We will update this article when MyNOURISH publishes, including if it finds nothing.</p>

