      <h2 id="where-it-fits">Where a collagen shot honestly fits</h2>
      <p>As protein and background micronutrients during a period when most people eat worse than usual, and nothing more. A fracture is a job for an orthopaedic team, not a supplement.</p>
      <p>Appetite drops after an injury or surgery, activity collapses, and older patients in particular arrive at the fracture already short on protein. That is the gap the Cochrane protein finding speaks to, and a 10g protein shot can help close it if your meals are not getting there. If they are, you do not need it.</p>
      <p>Two caveats. Collagen is not a complete protein and should not be counted as your whole intake — <a href="/pages/collagen-vs-whey-protein">collagen versus whey</a> explains why they do different jobs. And the dose question applies to collagen itself: the trial people quote used 5g of specific collagen peptides daily for 12 months and measured bone <em>density</em> in postmenopausal women, not fracture healing, so <a href="/pages/how-much-collagen-per-day">the doses the trials actually used</a> is the honest reference point.</p>

