      <h2 id="doctor">When to call your surgeon, not your chemist</h2>
      <p>Some fracture problems are time-critical, and a supplement question is the wrong thing to be researching while they develop. Get seen the same day if pain in a plastered or splinted limb is severe and worsening rather than settling, if the limb goes numb or tingling spreads, if the fingers or toes beyond the cast turn pale, blue or cold, if you cannot move them, or if the cast feels unbearably tight. Those are warning signs of compartment syndrome or a circulation problem, and they are emergencies.</p>
      <p>Call the team promptly, too, if a wound discharges or smells, if you develop fever and chills after fixation surgery, if the limb suddenly deforms or gives way, or if a new swollen, painful calf appears. And if pain and movement have simply stopped improving by the point your surgeon told you to expect progress, say so at the next review rather than adding another bottle — delayed union is diagnosed on films and examination, not on how you feel about your diet. Everything here is nutrition alongside proper orthopaedic care, never a substitute for it.</p>

