      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help a broken bone heal faster?</summary>
          <p>There is no trial showing that it does. A 2026 narrative review in the Journal of Dietary Supplements searched the fracture-healing literature and reported that no adult trials directly assess collagen. The first registered trial in fracture patients, MyNOURISH, expects to complete in 2027. Anyone telling you otherwise is extrapolating from bone density studies or from animal work.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What should I eat to heal a fracture faster?</summary>
          <p>Prioritise protein, then make sure calcium and vitamin D are covered. In Indian kitchens that means dal, curd, paneer, eggs, fish or chicken at every meal, with ragi, sesame and green leafy vegetables for calcium. High-protein supplementation after hip fracture reduced unfavourable outcomes in the Cochrane review, relative risk 0.78, which is the strongest nutrition finding in this field.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does hadjod really work for fractures?</summary>
          <p>The human evidence is two very small studies in facial bones. The pilot published in AYU in 2015 had five patients on Cissus quadrangularis and four controls, using 500mg three times a day for six weeks, and reported less pain and swelling with healing further along by day 21. That is nine patients in total, so it is a promising signal rather than proof, and most products contain well under the 1,500mg daily dose those studies used.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take calcium and vitamin D supplements after a fracture?</summary>
          <p>Ask your surgeon, and get a vitamin D level rather than guessing. Deficiency is linked to delayed and failed union, and 72% of Indian hip fracture patients were deficient in a 2026 cross-sectional study. But a randomised trial in JBMR Plus in 2023 found no healing benefit from vitamin D3 in women who were not deficient, so the point is correcting a real deficiency, not adding a supplement on principle.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take Skelecoll while my fracture is healing?</summary>
          <p>Tell your treating doctor what you are taking and follow their answer. Skelecoll is a 10g protein shot with 250mg calcium and 600 IU vitamin D3, so it is a nutritional background rather than a treatment, and it is not a fracture therapy. If you have been prescribed calcium or vitamin D at a treatment dose, your doctor needs to know about the 250mg and 600 IU so the totals are counted.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does smoking really affect bone healing?</summary>
          <p>Yes, and the effect is larger than anything nutritional in this article. A 2016 systematic review with meta-analysis found smokers carry roughly twice the risk of nonunion after fracture, osteotomy or arthrodesis, odds ratio 2.50 with a 95% confidence interval of 1.73 to 3.61. In conservatively treated displaced midshaft clavicle fractures the pooled risk ratio was 3.68. Stopping while the bone heals is the highest-value change available.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen the same thing as the collagen used in fracture surgery?</summary>
          <p>No. A randomised trial in The Journal of Bone and Joint Surgery in 1997 tested a bovine collagen and calcium phosphate graft material packed into acute fractures against the patient's own hip bone graft, and found no significant difference in union rates. That is an implanted surgical material. It carries no implication for a collagen supplement you drink.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will collagen stop me breaking another bone?</summary>
          <p>No trial has measured fracture rates on collagen. The nearest evidence is a 12-month randomised trial in which 5g of specific collagen peptides daily raised bone mineral density at the spine and femoral neck in postmenopausal women, and density is a risk marker rather than a fracture outcome. Weight-bearing exercise, protein, correcting vitamin D and any prescribed osteoporosis medicine remain the things that change fracture risk.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

